{"id": "doc-011", "type": "file", "content": "OAuth 2.0 Security Best Current Practice — Refresh Token Protection\n\nAuthorization servers MUST utilize one of these methods to detect refresh token replay by malicious actors for public clients:\n\nSender-constrained refresh tokens: the authorization server cryptographically binds the refresh token to a particular client instance.\n\nRefresh token rotation: the authorization server issues a new refresh token with every access token refresh response. The previous refresh token is invalidated but information about the relationship is retained by the authorization server. If a refresh token is compromised and subsequently used by both the attacker and the legitimate client, one of them will present an invalidated refresh token, which will inform the authorization server of the breach. The authorization server cannot determine which party is legitimate, so MUST revoke the active refresh token family.", "user_note": "인증 서버 구현할 때 참고", "application": "Preview", "window_title": "draft-ietf-oauth-security-topics.pdf", "url": null, "captured_at": "2026-08-18T11:30:00+09:00", "summary": null, "topics": []}
